import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="First article: Astro pages that pass every SEO rule"
  description="A nested route showing that directory traversal, canonical URL detection and the heading-outline check all keep working below the site root."
>
  <h1>Writing pages that pass every SEO rule</h1>
  <p>
    This article lives at a nested path, so the recursive directory traversal in
    the integration is exercised on more than just the site root. The canonical
    URL, the heading outline and the internal-link resolver all have to work the
    same way one level down as they do at the top of the tree.
  </p>
  <h2>The short checklist</h2>
  <p>
    Give the page a descriptive title in the thirty to sixty character range and
    a meta description between fifty and one hundred and sixty characters. Use
    exactly one top-level heading and never skip a level on the way down. Emit an
    absolute canonical URL, which Astro can build for you once the
    <code>site</code> option is set. Wrap the page in real landmarks so assistive
    technology and the semantic-HTML rule both find a <code>main</code> region.
  </p>
  <h2>Content that earns the ranking</h2>
  <p>
    A page also needs enough of its own writing to be worth indexing. Programmatic
    pages fail here most often: a city name or a product attribute is swapped into
    an otherwise identical template and the result is a hundred pages that read as
    one. The thin-content rule and the unique-ratio pass of the near-duplicate
    check are both aimed at that pattern, so this article carries several
    paragraphs that appear nowhere else on the site.
  </p>
  <h2>Staying in the link graph</h2>
  <p>
    Finally, every page should be reachable. This one is linked from the primary
    navigation and from the body of two other routes, and it appears in the XML
    sitemap that the build generates. Continue to the
    <a href="/about/">about page for this demo</a> or head back to the
    <a href="/">home page</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
